                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1274-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>Audio-Technica ATM31</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The ATM31 is a vocal condenser microphone with a fixed-charge electret capsule. It requires the use of a 1.5V &#8220;AA&#8221; battery, which fits into the 21mm shaft of the microphone body (where it would reportedly last for several thousand hours of usage).</p><p>The electret capsule is a small-diaphragm design with a 4-micron, low-mass diaphragm. </p><p>The mic body houses the amplifier circuit, the aforementioned AA/UM3 battery compartment, a power switch, and, according to the circuit diagram, a very small transformer that steps the mic&#8217;s output impedance down from 2000 to 600&Omega;. </p><p>According to the owner&#8217;s manual, this head amplifier can be used with other electret-capsule heads, although no specific models are named.<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Audio-Technica/ATM31">Audio-Technica ATM31</a></p>
